    {
      "reference": "3.37",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τῶν διὰ σὲ κτισθέντων δι’ εὐγνωμοσύνην τὸ διαμένειν ἐχόντων",
          "english_quote": "with the things created for your sake continuing to exist because of your gratitude",
          "issue": "The Greek does not expressly identify whose εὐγνωμοσύνη sustains the created things. The draft adopts human gratitude as the cause and places the genitive absolute within the hypothetical situation. Another material reading takes the created things' continuance through their own gratitude or good disposition as the comparison underlying 'you too would be immortal.' The note does not preserve this alternative or explain the choice between them.",
          "suggested_revision": "Either retain the contextual human-gratitude reading with a note explaining its hypothetical attachment and preserving the comparative reading, or use the less determinate 'with the things created for your sake possessing continued existence through gratitude,' accompanied by the alternatives. Review the revised wording and note afresh.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐὰν ἐπιγνῷς ὃν ἔλαθες",
          "english_quote": "if you recognize the one you failed to notice",
          "issue": "The rendering requires an extended 'overlook/forget' sense of the active ἔλαθες. Its ordinary active construction instead means that the subject escaped the object's notice: 'the one whose notice you escaped.' The contextual forget/overlook interpretation is defensible, but no note explains this semantic choice or records the materially different ordinary construction.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted lexical note explaining the adopted forget/overlook sense and its contextual basis, while preserving the ordinary active alternative. Do not describe the verified printed wording as an OCR defect or silently replace it with a different construction.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "3.38",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ νῦν δὲ μετὰ πάντων",
          "english_quote": "Even now, before everyone,",
          "issue": "μετὰ πάντων expresses association with everyone, not expressly an appearance before an audience. The draft replaces that association with an audience relationship without explaining the attachment.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'Even now, with everyone,' or, if the intended contextual interpretation is everyone's presence, 'Even now, with everyone present,' with a note preserving the possible association with the discussion itself.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "περὶ τοῦ δεῖν θεοὺς νομίζειν",
          "english_quote": "about whether gods ought to be acknowledged.",
          "issue": "The articular infinitive presents the necessity or obligation to acknowledge gods as the discussion's subject. 'Whether' introduces an open alternative that is not expressed here and weakens Simon's stated position following his appeal to books that affirm many gods.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'about the need to acknowledge gods' or 'about why gods must be acknowledged.' The first is the more source-transparent candidate and avoids adding an explanatory claim.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀπρόγνωστός ἐστιν",
          "english_quote": "since he lacks foreknowledge",
          "issue": "The contextual active interpretation is supported by the subsequent contrast with προγνωστικός and the accusations concerning ignorance. Nevertheless, the adjective also admits the formally passive reading 'not known beforehand.' The existing note supplies only synonymous active renderings and does not disclose that alternative or explain why the context favors the active interpretation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain 'lacks foreknowledge' if desired, but expand the note to record 'not known beforehand' and explain the choice through the explicit contrast with προγνωστικός and the following examples. This is interpretive uncertainty in verified wording, not a source-verification block.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "3.39",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": false,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ δὲ ἐπαγαγεῖν „μήπως φαγὼν ζήσεται εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα“ καὶ φθονεῖ",
          "english_quote": "while his further statement, “perhaps, after eating, he will live forever,” indicates envy as well.",
          "issue": "The repeated quotation retains μήπως from the preventive statement about expelling Adam. Rendering it simply 'perhaps' drops that apprehensive/preventive force, which supplies the connection to envy. The note's appeal to Simon isolating the word applies to the preceding quotation of μήπως alone; here he quotes the word together with the eating-and-living clause. That explanation therefore does not justify the loss in this quotation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'while his addition, “lest perhaps, after eating, he live forever,” also indicates envy.' Revise the note to distinguish uncertainty expressed by the isolated μήπως from the preventive force retained in the repeated clause. This proposed correction requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "3.40",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "αὐτὸν μόνον ἀγαθὸν ὄντα",
          "english_quote": "though he alone is good",
          "issue": "The draft defensibly attaches μόνον to αὐτόν, making God the sole good one. The same printed form can also function adverbially with ἀγαθόν, yielding 'being only good' or 'being nothing but good.' That alternative concerns the absence of evil in God rather than the exclusion of goodness from everyone else. The existing note does not disclose this material theological attachment ambiguity.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a note preserving both 'he alone is good' and 'he is nothing but good,' explaining why the chosen attachment is favored. The contrast with 'evil and wicked' supports the latter possibility, while the surrounding insistence on divine uniqueness supports the former. No source re-verification is required merely because both readings remain viable.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
